{"name":"Eternal Summer","hex":"#f7e504","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"eternal","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Lasting forever; unending.","Existing outside time; as opposed to sempiternal, existing within time but everlastingly."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One who lives forever; an immortal."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/eternal"},{"term":"summer","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of four seasons, traditionally the second, marked by the longest and typically hottest days of the year due to the inclination of the Earth and thermal lag. Typically regarded as spanning  either the period between the summer solstice to the autumnal equinox, or the months of June, July, and August in the Northern Hemisphere and the months of December, January and February in the Southern Hemisphere.","year; used to give the age of a person, usually a young one."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To spend the summer, as in a particular place on holiday."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/summer"}],"entries":[{"title":"Eternal Summer (2006 film)","blocks":[{"text":"Eternal Summer is a 2006 Taiwanese film starring Joseph Chang, Ray Chang and Kate Yeung. It was directed by Leste Chen. The film tells the story of three high school students who experience the agonies and ecstasies of love."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/9d/Eternal_Summer_poster.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eternal_Summer_(2006_film)","wikidata":"Q639927"},{"title":"Summer","blocks":[{"text":"Summer or summertime is the hottest and brightest of the four temperate seasons, occurring after spring and before autumn. Starting on the summer solstice, daylight hours are the longest and darkness hours are the shortest, with day length decreasing as the season progresses after the solstice. In the Northern Hemisphere, summer is usually defined as starting on June 21 or 22, and ending on September 22 or 23; and in the Southern Hemisphere, as the period between December 22 or 23 and March 20 or 21."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c4/Field_Hamois_Belgium_Luc_Viatour.jpg/330px-Field_Hamois_Belgium_Luc_Viatour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Summer","wikidata":"Q1313"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Inari%2C_Finland_-_panoramio_%285%29.jpg/500px-Inari%2C_Finland_-_panoramio_%285%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Inari%2C_Finland_-_panoramio_%285%29.jpg/1280px-Inari%2C_Finland_-_panoramio_%285%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/63/Inari%2C_Finland_-_panoramio_%285%29.jpg/500px-Inari%2C_Finland_-_panoramio_%285%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"During summer in the polar regions, the sun can appear even at midnight. Photo of midnight sun in Inari, Finland.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AInari%2C_Finland_-_panoramio_(5).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Darwin_thunderstorm.jpg/500px-Darwin_thunderstorm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f0/Darwin_thunderstorm.jpg/500px-Darwin_thunderstorm.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Wet season thunderstorm at night in Darwin, Northern Territory, Australia.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADarwin_thunderstorm.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fd/Ida_2021-08-29_1321Z.jpg/500px-Ida_2021